WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.457
Picture the restaurant opening a second branch, then a third.

2
00:00:06.457 --> 00:00:11.076
The quickest plan is to photocopy the recipe binder, once for each kitchen.

3
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.639
It works for a month.

4
00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.317
Then one branch cuts the salt in the soup.

5
00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.229
Another changes the oven time.

6
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:22.172
A year later, the three soups taste different.

7
00:00:22.172 --> 00:00:26.636
And nobody can say which changes were on purpose, and which were accidents.

8
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.871
Our app is in the same spot.

9
00:00:28.871 --> 00:00:34.455
hello-devops has over ten YAML files, plain text files describing its objects.

10
00:00:34.455 --> 00:00:37.319
Dev, staging and prod make three copies.

11
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:42.901
Each copy needs its own image tag, its own replica count and its own greeting.

12
00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:45.653
And prod also wants a disruption budget.

13
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:49.329
Then someone fixes a probe in prod, and forgets dev.

14
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:52.066
Copy and paste is a wonderful invention.

15
00:00:52.066 --> 00:00:56.309
It makes three things that agree today, and disagree forever after.

16
00:00:56.533 --> 00:01:01.313
What we want is to write the recipe once, and say only what differs per branch.

17
00:01:01.313 --> 00:01:02.947
There are two popular ways.

18
00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:07.338
The first way is a recipe card with blanks for the amounts.

19
00:01:07.338 --> 00:01:10.244
You write the YAML once, and leave blanks in it.

20
00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:13.513
This blank reads the replica count from the values.

21
00:01:13.513 --> 00:01:17.157
This one reads the greeting, and the pipe wraps it in quotes.

22
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:22.137
The chart comes with default settings, the values that fill the blanks.

23
00:01:22.137 --> 00:01:23.010
Two replicas.

24
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.085
Hello from Helm.

25
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.186
Each branch adds its own values file on top.

26
00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:30.466
The lab's file for kind changes only the greeting.

27
00:01:30.466 --> 00:01:33.877
When two files set the same key, the later one wins.

28
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.945
Fill the blanks, and out comes plain YAML.

29
00:01:36.945 --> 00:01:40.535
Two replicas, and hello from Helm on kind, in quotes.

30
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:46.344
The tool is Helm, which installs packaged apps, a bit like an app store for a cluster.

31
00:01:46.344 --> 00:01:49.847
Its package is a chart: templates plus default values.

32
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:55.324
And helm template fills every blank and prints the result, without touching the cluster.

33
00:01:55.324 --> 00:01:56.757
Look before you install.

34
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:02.296
When you install a chart, Helm creates a release, one named copy of it.

35
00:02:02.296 --> 00:02:08.225
And every install, upgrade or rollback adds a numbered revision, like lines in a logbook.

36
00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:09.926
Say revision 3 is bad.

37
00:02:09.926 --> 00:02:12.166
You run helm rollback, back to 1.

38
00:02:12.166 --> 00:02:15.899
Pause and guess: what does the history show afterwards?

39
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.157
A brand new revision 4, with the same content as revision 1.

40
00:02:22.157 --> 00:02:25.132
Revisions 2 and 3 stay right where they are.

41
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:28.328
Helm keeps its logbook in pen, not pencil.

42
00:02:28.328 --> 00:02:30.253
History is never rewritten.

43
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:34.012
Now a trap that catches nearly everyone.

44
00:02:34.012 --> 00:02:35.927
Defaults say two replicas.

45
00:02:35.927 --> 00:02:39.167
You install with a prod file that says five.

46
00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:39.830
Five run.

47
00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.935
So far so good.

48
00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:44.540
Later you upgrade, passing only a new image tag.

49
00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.141
Surely Helm remembers your prod file.

50
00:02:47.141 --> 00:02:48.687
How many replicas now?

51
00:02:48.687 --> 00:02:49.812
Pause and guess.

52
00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:52.384
Two.

53
00:02:52.384 --> 00:02:52.999
Well...

54
00:02:52.999 --> 00:02:55.808
that's not the number we wanted.

55
00:02:56.033 --> 00:03:03.099
The rule: pass any values on an upgrade, and Helm starts from the chart's defaults plus only what you pass now.

56
00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:05.455
Install-time settings quietly vanish.

57
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:10.303
So keep settings in a values file, and pass it with dash f every time.

58
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:13.783
The second way starts from the other end.

59
00:03:13.783 --> 00:03:17.660
Keep the recipe as plain, valid YAML, with no blanks at all.

60
00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.921
That's the base, the master recipe.

61
00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:23.660
You've used it already, with kubectl apply dash k.

62
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:25.681
An overlay is another folder.

63
00:03:25.681 --> 00:03:29.749
It points at the base, and lists only the changes for one branch.

64
00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:31.439
Those are the sticky notes.

65
00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:41.956
Prod moves everything into the namespace hello-prod, runs four replicas, says hello from prod, and adds one file only prod has: a disruption budget.

66
00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:46.006
Kustomize lays the notes on top, and prints the result.

67
00:03:46.006 --> 00:03:47.193
Plain YAML again.

68
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.023
Lift the notes off, and the base is untouched.

69
00:03:51.023 --> 00:03:53.332
Nothing in the k8s folder changed.

70
00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:56.008
Lay down the dev notes instead.

71
00:03:56.008 --> 00:04:01.995
Namespace hello-dev, one replica, a newer image, 1.1.0, and hello from dev.

72
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.700
One base, two small overlays, running side by side.

73
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.680
The tool is built into kubectl, and it's called Kustomize.

74
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.608
One trap: put an overlay inside its own base folder, and Kustomize refuses.

75
00:04:15.608 --> 00:04:16.750
Cycle detected.

76
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.947
Overlays sit next to k8s, never inside it.

77
00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:22.658
Two roads, one destination.

78
00:04:22.658 --> 00:04:29.804
Either way, the result is ordinary Kubernetes YAML, and the API server never knows which tool made it.

79
00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:30.957
So which one?

80
00:04:30.957 --> 00:04:33.016
Mostly, whose software is it?

81
00:04:33.016 --> 00:04:37.136
Someone else's, like Envoy Gateway, ships as a Helm chart.

82
00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.385
Your own app, a few differences?

83
00:04:39.385 --> 00:04:42.223
Either works, and Kustomize stays plain YAML.

84
00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:44.204
Many teams, many settings?

85
00:04:44.204 --> 00:04:47.268
Helm's values give each team one clear place.

86
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.877
And each has a limit.

87
00:04:48.877 --> 00:04:53.140
A values file that repeats the whole Deployment has gone too far.

88
00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.205
An overlay that changes half the base means the base is wrong.

89
00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:00.842
The moral of the story: photocopies drift.

90
00:05:00.842 --> 00:05:03.316
Write it once, say only what differs.

91
00:05:03.316 --> 00:05:09.335
Helm is a recipe card with blanks and a logbook in pen; pass your values on every upgrade.

92
00:05:09.335 --> 00:05:12.478
Kustomize is a master recipe with sticky notes.

93
00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.685
The cluster only sees plain YAML.

94
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:16.529
One quick check.

95
00:05:16.529 --> 00:05:22.829
Prod needs a bigger memory limit on the base Deployment's web container, and nothing else changes.

96
00:05:22.829 --> 00:05:25.143
What do you add to the prod overlay?

97
00:05:25.143 --> 00:05:26.172
Pause and think.

98
00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.085
A small patch naming the Deployment and the web container, with only the changed fields.

99
00:05:34.085 --> 00:05:36.088
A sticky note, not a photocopy.

100
00:05:36.088 --> 00:05:37.380
Thanks for watching.
